=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The `bgpd` daemon (and its supervisor `watchfrr`) on router1 is not running. Only `zebra` is alive on that device.

**Mechanism:** In this simple-BGP topology, router1 (AS side for the 195.11.14.0/24 prefix) and router2 (AS 2, advertising 200.1.1.0/24) rely entirely on a BGP eBGP session to exchange reachability information. With `bgpd` dead on router1, no TCP session can be established on port 179. router2's BGP neighbor is permanently stuck in the `Active` state — it keeps trying to connect and failing. As a result, neither router has a learned route for the other's host subnet: router1 has no route to 200.1.1.0/24, and router2 never receives an advertisement for 195.11.14.0/24. Both local gateways therefore return ICMP `Destination Net Unreachable` when pc1 or pc2 tries to ping the remote host, producing 100% packet loss in both directions.

**Localization:** router1, all interfaces — the fault is process-level, not interface-level. zebra is running (kernel FIB management works), but bgpd is absent.

**Root cause:** The BGP daemon (`bgpd`) has crashed or been stopped on router1, collapsing the only inter-AS routing channel and making 200.1.1.0/24 unreachable from pc1 and 195.11.14.0/24 unreachable from pc2.

**Fix:** Restart FRR on router1 (`service frr restart` or `systemctl restart frr`). This will bring up `bgpd` and `watchfrr`, the BGP session with router2 will re-establish, routes will be exchanged, and full pc1↔pc2 connectivity will be restored. **Do not restart router2** — it is fully healthy.
=== END ===